{"chunk_id": "d504705d9d85", "text": "Krishna describes the discipline of meditation and devotion (yoga) that leads to union with the divine. The true devotee is one who has mastered the senses and treats all beings\u2014friends, enemies, strangers, good and wicked\u2014with equal vision. Krishna explains practical meditation: sitting steadily in a clean place, controlling the body and mind, focusing attention inward, and practicing moderation in food, sleep, and activity. When the mind becomes steady like a lamp in a windless place, the practitioner experiences a happiness beyond the senses and becomes free from sorrow. Arjuna objects that the mind is too restless to control, but Krishna replies that it can be mastered through practice and detachment. Even failed efforts are not wasted; spiritual progress carries into future lives. Ultimately, the highest path is devotion\u2014seeing the divine in all beings and all beings in the divine.\nKrishna teaches disciplined meditation and equanimity, assures that failed yogis are not lost, and declares devoted union with the divine the highest path.\nThemes: meditation discipline, control of mind, equanimity toward all beings, perseverance across rebirths, supremacy of devotion", "vector": [0.005335281603038311, 0.00642757024616003, 0.02223089337348938, -0.003751629265025258, -0.008665267378091812, 0.010431521572172642, -0.015936125069856644, -0.024833794683218002, -0.017025092616677284, -0.008791428990662098, -0.007430218160152435, 0.017011811956763268, 0.02223089337348938, 0.017317255958914757, -0.008857829496264458, 0.009149990975856781, 0.058166854083538055, -0.01072368398308754, 0.02815381996333599, -0.0015728622674942017, -0.006294769234955311, 0.008930869400501251, 0.001806094078347087, -0.014222990721464157, -0.014634674414992332, 0.02127472683787346, 0.009408953599631786, -0.02855222299695015, -0.0054315621964633465, -0.019813913851976395, 0.0022493174765259027, 0.014634674414992332, 0.00022036672453396022, 0.0058